WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.200
A differential equation specifies a rate in terms of the unknown quantity

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.800
Here the rate is negative twice the current value

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:16.000
Starting at three, a positive solution decreases quickly at first and more slowly as its value approaches zero.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.174
For a positive candidate solution, divide by y and integrate

00:00:22.174 --> 00:00:32.409
The derivative of the logarithm is y prime divided by y, so the logarithm equals negative twice time plus a constant

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:35.333
Positivity justifies this particular logarithm expression.

00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:39.826
Exponentiating gives a constant times the exponential

00:00:39.826 --> 00:00:43.475
The initial value sets that constant to three

00:00:43.475 --> 00:00:52.142
Differentiate the proposed formula to check the rate equation, and substitute time zero separately to check the initial condition

00:00:52.142 --> 00:00:53.967
Both checks are necessary.

00:00:55.267 --> 00:00:59.682
The final formula also works for negative initial values

00:00:59.682 --> 00:01:07.041
The identically zero solution must be checked directly because dividing by y excluded it earlier

00:01:07.041 --> 00:01:14.400
Every real initial value approaches the zero equilibrium, illustrating stability for this particular linear equation.
